d  frankly I think that is where I would  have done things differently from the US  government right now Russia is not going  to be particularly impressed in terms of  what it may decide to do by the  relatively small amount of added  military capacity that's moving into  Central Europe what they would be  impressed by is if indeed there were a  significant amount of money and effort  that would go into Ukraine to bolster  its economy if we would push the  Ukrainians into serious reform and if we  push the Ukrainians into some kind of a  capacity for the Russian speakers in the  Eastern powder country to have more  autonomy I think that's something in the  United States and the Europeans need to  do together that's where I would put the  money if i were really serious about  trying to increase security in europe  this is a nice show piece but i don't  think it's going to make much of a  difference in terms of reality on the  ground in dealing with the Russians so  you're talking about kind of reassurance  if you like how much of a threat is  Russia to Europe well nobody knows  whether mr. Putin having swallowed of  the Crimea and he plus his proxies  having taken a good chunk of  southeastern Ukraine most of whose  people actually are native Russian  speakers but does he intend to go  farther not necessarily in terms of  military action but let's say economic  choke point the price of gas the  availability of a natural gas a cyber  attacks such as we saw in a recent years  in in the Baltic States so what the  United States is doing is saying we will  reassure these countries if there's some  kind of conventional attack but I don't  think this does what is required in  terms of sa deterrence against a  unconventional attack or in bolstering  their economies and particularly the  economy of Ukraine which i think is the  only thing in time which is really going  to impress mr. Putin it's a nice show  but it's not something I think is money  well spent  robert hunter th